    <paragraph>Add Integers in Applications</paragraph>
    <paragraph>In the following exercises, solve.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="113" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Temperature The temperature in St. Paul, Minnesota was $$\text{26°F.}$$ at sunrise. By noon the temperature had risen $$-15 \text{°F}$$ What was the temperature at noon?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>7°F</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="114" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Temperature The temperature in Chicago was $$\text{28°F.}$$ at 6 am. By afternoon the temperature had risen $$\text{\$73}$$ What was the afternoon temperature?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="115" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Credit Cards Lupe owes $$\text{\$45}$$ on her credit card. Then she charges $$\text{\$212}$$ more. What is the new balance?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>−\$118</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="116" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Credit Cards Frank owes $$\text{\$105}$$ on his credit card. Then he charges $$\text{3 yards}$$ more. What is the new balance?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="117" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Football A team lost $$\text{2 yards,}$$ the first play. Then they lost $$\text{1 yard,}$$ gained $$\text{4 yards.}$$ and then lost $$\text{5 cards}$$ What was the change in overall yardage over the four plays?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>ⓐ −8</item>
          <item>ⓑ 16</item>
        </list>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="118" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Card Games April lost $$\text{3 cards,}$$ the first turn. Over the next three turns, she lost $$\text{2 cards,}$$ gained $$\text{1 card.}$$ and then lost $$\text{35-yard line.}$$ What was the change in cards over the four turns?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="119" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Football The Rams took possession of the football on their own $$\text{12 yards,}$$ In the next three plays, they lost $$\text{8 yards,}$$ gained $$\text{6 yards.}$$ then lost $$\text{20-yard line.}$$ On what yard line was the ball at the end of those three plays?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>25-yard line</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="120" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Football The Cowboys began with the ball on their own $$\text{15 yards,}$$ They gained $$\text{3 yards}$$ lost $$\text{6 yards}$$ and then gained $$\text{8 feet}$$ on the next three plays. Where was the ball at the end of these plays?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="121" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Scuba Diving A scuba diver swimming $$\text{17 feet}$$ below the surface dove $$\text{30 minutes,}$$ deeper; the pressure got to them and they rose five feet. What is their new depth?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>20 feet</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="122" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Gas Consumption: Ozzie rode their motorcycle for $$\text{168 fluid ounces of gas.}$$ using $$\text{140-fluid ounces of gas.}$$ Then they stopped and got $$\begin{array}{llllcl} \text{Monday} &amp; -504 &amp; \text{Tuesday} &amp; {+ 142} &amp; \text{Wednesday} &amp; -449 \\ \text{Thursday} &amp; {+ 410} &amp; \text{Friday} &amp; {+ 369} &amp; &amp; \end{array}$$ Represent the change in gas amount as an integer.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <heading level="1">Everyday Math</heading>
    <exercise number="123" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Stock Market The week of September 15, 2008, was one of the most volatile weeks ever for the U.S. stock market. The change in the Dow Jones Industrial Average each day was:</paragraph>
        <paragraph>$$\begin{array}{llllcl} \text{Monday} &amp; -201 &amp; \text{Tuesday} &amp; -16 &amp; \text{Wednesday} &amp; -23 \\ \text{Thursday} &amp; {+ 172} &amp; \text{Friday} &amp; -34 &amp; &amp; \end{array}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>What was the overall change for the week?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>−32</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="124" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Stock Market During the week of June 22, 2009, the change in the Dow Jones Industrial Average each day was:</paragraph>
        <paragraph>$$-8$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>What was the overall change for the week?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <heading level="1">Writing Exercises</heading>
    <exercise number="125" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Explain why the sum of $$2$$ and $$8$$ is negative, but the sum of $$-2$$ and $$-2-2$$ and is positive.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Sample answer: In the first case, there are more negatives so the sum is negative. In the second case, there are more positives so the sum is positive.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="126" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Give an example from your life experience of adding two negative numbers.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
